<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pentecost</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Whit Sunday, Whitsunday, Whitsun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pentecost is a Christian holiday held on the 49th day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> (the 50th by inclusive counting), commemorating the descent of the Holy Spirit upon the Apostles and other followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> during the Feast of Weeks, as described in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koine-greek">Koine Greek</a> pentēkostē, fiftieth, a word the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> used for Shavuot, the Jewish harvest festival held on the fiftieth day after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Passover</a>. It is called the birthday of the Church. As its date depends on Easter it is a moveable feast; it is one of the Great feasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> and a Solemnity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, and the Monday after it is a legal holiday in many countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pentecost.jpg" alt="Pentecost" width="330" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ingeborg_Psalter_02f_1200_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The oculus has never been covered, so rain falls on the floor, which slopes about 30 centimetres to its drains; at <b>Pentecost</b> firefighters throw red rose petals through it from the roof.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A &quot;Missa greca&quot; sung at <b>Pentecost</b> survives in a sacramentary written for canonesses at Essen, with the Greek words in Latin letters and early neumes, and the Royal Abbey of Saint-Denis sang the hymn in Greek and in Latin at Pentecost and in its patron's festal week, after…</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Catholic Church sings it as the responsorial psalm after the creation reading of the Easter Vigil, and again at <b>Pentecost</b>; the Book of Common Prayer appoints it for the evening of the twentieth day of the month and for Evensong on Whitsunday.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104</a></li><li><blockquote>As an invocation of the Holy Spirit, the hymn is sung in the Catholic Church at <b>Pentecost</b>, at Terce and Vespers.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Missal of the Tridentine Mass, published in 1570 after the council, kept only four medieval sequences: this one, &quot;Veni Sancte Spiritus&quot; for <b>Pentecost</b>, &quot;Lauda Sion&quot; for Corpus Christi and &quot;Dies irae&quot; for the Requiem Mass.</blockquote><a href="/songs/victimae-paschali-laudes">Victimae paschali laudes</a></li></ul></section>
